  <section aria-labelledby="accessibility-tools">
    <h2 id="accessibility-tools">Accessibility Tools</h2>

    <p>
      Much of my work is about making tools usable without sight. Here is a
      command-line tool I built so that a screen reader user can do the same
      acoustic phonetics work as everyone else.
    </p>

    <ul class="project-list" aria-label="Accessibility tools">
      <li class="project-card">
        <span class="tag">Accessibility</span>
        <h3>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/jacob-torres/praatshell"
            rel="external noopener"
          >
            praatshell
          </a>
        </h3>
        <p>
          A screen-reader-first command shell for phonetic analysis, written in
          Python on top of Praat's own analysis engine (through the
          parselmouth library).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The accessible use case:</strong> Praat is the standard free
          program for acoustic phonetics, but nearly all of it happens in
          graphical windows. The waveform and the spectrogram are pictures with
          no spoken equivalent, so a screen reader cannot get at them, and a
          student who uses one is locked out of the coursework. praatshell makes
          the same measurements through Praat's engine and reports them as
          text. You type a command such as <code>describe</code>,
          <code>vowels</code> or <code>spectrogram</code>, and it answers in a
          sentence or two, with the full detail written to a report file and
          CSVs you can read at your own pace.
        </p>
        <p>
          It divides a recording into regions and says in words what the
          waveform is doing: whether it is periodic, how its loudness rises and
          falls, and where its energy sits in frequency. It describes
          spectrograms and power spectra in prose, with formants, harmonics,
          spectral moments and a choice of window width, and it tables
          vowel durations and voice onset times. I checked its measurements
          against my own hand measurements in Praat, and they agree to within a
          few milliseconds.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
